[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
This is Orca's server (orcad, what upstream's `orca serve` runs) for
FreeBSD. The UI runs elsewhere: in the Orca desktop app on Linux, macOS
or Windows, or in any web browser. Terminals, agents and repositories
stay on this machine.

Run it as a service:

  sysrc orca_ide_enable=YES
  sysrc orca_ide_pairing_address=<address the client dials>
  service orca_ide start
  service orca_ide pair

`service orca_ide pair` prints two links:

- "Pairing URL": in the desktop app, open Settings -> Remote Orca
  Servers -> Add Server and paste it.
- "Web client URL": open it in a browser on the client machine.

Each link pairs one client; run `service orca_ide pair` again for the
next. Treat the links like passwords. Paired clients are revoked in
Settings -> Remote Orca Servers -> Shared Server Access.

Or run it in the foreground as yourself, which prints the same links:

  orca-ide serve --pairing-address <address the client dials>

The server listens on TCP port 6768 (orca_ide_port) on every interface
(orca_ide_bind). Clients need to reach that port. Keep it on a private
network path (LAN, Tailscale, WireGuard or an SSH tunnel) and do not
expose it to the Internet.

The service runs as user "orca" (home %%ORCA_HOME%%). Agents, git and
terminals run with that user's home, credentials and files. To use
your own repositories and agent logins, set orca_ide_user to your own
user instead. Install and log in to the agent CLIs (claude, codex, ...)
as that user. Orca's CLI is `orca-ide`, for example
`orca-ide account add --agent claude`. Point it at the service's profile
with ORCA_USER_DATA when it is not ~/.orca.

`service orca_ide stop` stops the server but, as upstream intends, not
its terminal daemon. Terminals and running agents survive restarts.

Not available on FreeBSD:
- the listening-ports view of a workspace;
- per-terminal memory and process limits (they use systemd on Linux);
- server-side browser panes (upstream drives them with Electron or a
  Linux-only helper); pages can still open on the client;
- speech to text, and computer use (`orca computer`);
- `orca serve service install` (use the rc script) and self-update
  (use pkg).
EOM
}
]
